from deliver import add,D,K
from library import *
from noncrossref import rafler
D.clear();K.update(excitable='10.1137/0134040',organization='10.1117/12.485805',dla='10.1103/PhysRevLett.47.1400')
add('smoothlife', '''Which features of Life-like organization survive when binary states become continuous values? NetLogo SmoothLife averages a patch field over an inner disk and surrounding annulus, then uses smooth sigmoid transitions to calculate the next generation. Rafler's original preprint [1] supplies the direct rule provenance, Wolfram [2] provides cellular-automaton context, and Fisch and colleagues [3] connect neighborhood thresholds with excitable pattern formation. The NetLogo Info explicitly retains discrete time and approximates continuous space with a finite grid. It also omits very small state contributions as a numerical optimization. Radius, transition widths, and birth and survival intervals affect whether moving structures persist. The simulation illustrates a parameterized generalization of local rule-driven organization; organic appearance does not identify living behavior, and a discretized update is not an exact simulation of every continuous formulation.''', [('rafler',''),('wolfram','Cellular-automaton theory provides the discrete reference point for the continuous-valued generalization.'),('excitable2','Threshold and neighborhood-range studies give a related framework for interpreting pattern formation, not a claim that the rules are identical.')])
add('hex-cell-aggregation', '''How do neighborhood eligibility rules influence the shape of a growing cluster? NetLogo Hex Cell Aggregation starts with one occupied central cell on a nonwrapping hexagonal lattice. At each tick, one eligible empty cell becomes occupied, with eligibility determined by the chosen occupied-neighbor counts. Wolfram [1] supplies the local-rule growth context, Reiter [2] studies a different hexagonal cellular growth model, and Witten and Sander [3] provide a contrasting stochastic aggregation mechanism. These comparisons separate geometry and growth order from the physics that might motivate an application. The baseline has no diffusion field, nutrient transport, or crystallographic energy. Finite boundaries, random choice among eligible cells, and the selected neighbor counts shape the outcome. The experiment illustrates geometric growth constraints; resemblance to a crystal or colony does not make it a validated model of either.''', [('wolfram','Local cellular rules provide the theoretical framework for growing configurations.'),('hex','Hexagonal crystal-growth modeling offers a direct topology comparison while using different physical assumptions.'),('dla','Diffusion-limited aggregation is a scientifically important contrasting mechanism; NetLogo’s eligible-cell growth should not be mislabeled DLA.')])
add('vants', '''Can a deterministic rule appear irregular while remaining reversible? NetLogo Vants moves virtual ants across a binary patch field, toggling the visited color and turning right or left according to that color. Langton [1] provides the artificial-life context, Gale and colleagues [2] investigate generalized ant dynamics, and Turing [3] supplies the broader connection between local read-write rules and computation. The original NetLogo rule moves before examining and changing the new patch, so operation order is part of its identity. Its world wraps, and multiple ants share the same writable field. Initial headings, ant count, activation order, and finite boundaries affect the visible trace. The model illustrates how simple deterministic interactions can generate long transients and structured paths; apparent disorder is not evidence of stochasticity, and reversibility requires an explicitly defined inverse update.''', [('ant','Langton’s artificial-life research supplies the original rule-driven computational context credited by the model.'),('ant2','The original generalized-ant study directly examines turning rules and long-term trajectory organization.'),('turing','Read-write computational machines explain the conceptual relationship between a moving head and a mutable cell field.')])
add('turing-machine-2d', '''How can small read-write agents perform complicated computation on a shared field? NetLogo Turing Machine 2D extends the tape idea to a two-dimensional patch array and potentially many heads. Each head follows a transition table determined by its internal state and the current patch value, changing state, writing, and choosing a movement direction. Turing [1] provides the foundational computational formalism; Langton's self-reproduction study [2] and artificial-life work [3] connect local machines with spatial construction and collective patterns. The NetLogo baseline is a finite multihead teaching variant, not Turing's original one-dimensional tape. Shared writes and head activation order can affect interactions. An intricate picture does not prove universal computation or successful parallelism. The simulation illustrates explicit state transitions and the consequences of allowing several processors to modify one environment.''', [('turing','Foundational account of stateful read-write computation is the direct theoretical ancestor.'),('selfrep','Spatial self-reproduction by local computational rules supplies a relevant extension beyond a one-dimensional tape.'),('ant','Artificial-life cellular systems connect the moving-head mechanism to emergent spatial organization.')])
add('ca-1d-elementary', '''How many different behaviors can arise from the same tiny neighborhood? NetLogo CA 1D Elementary explores the 256 binary rules mapping a cell and its immediate left and right neighbors to one next-state bit. Each new row records the next generation, so the display combines space and time rather than representing a two-dimensional spatial population. Wolfram [1] supplies the foundational statistical study, Li and colleagues [2] compare transitions across rule space, and Chaté and Manneville [3] analyze critical behavior in cellular automata. Single-cell and random initializations expose sensitivity to starting conditions. Rule numbering, boundary wrapping, and continuation at the bottom are part of the baseline. Finite patterns may resemble chaos or nested structure, but visual classification alone does not prove randomness, universality, or correspondence with a measured natural process.''', [('wolfram','Original statistical mechanics treatment provides the elementary-automaton foundation.'),('ca1','Rule-space transitions directly motivate comparing qualitatively different elementary update tables.'),('ca2','Criticality research limits conclusions drawn from short visual runs or simple behavior classes.')])
add('ca-continuous', '''What changes when a cellular rule acts on real-valued states instead of bits? NetLogo CA Continuous averages a cell and its nearest neighbors, adds a chosen constant, and keeps the fractional part to obtain the next state. Values are plotted in successive rows as a space-time diagram. Kaneko [1] supplies the coupled-map-lattice context explicitly cited in the original Info, Wolfram [2] provides a discrete cellular comparison, and Peitgen and colleagues [3] explain iterated-map and numerical-chaos concepts. The precision control quantizes the nominally continuous values, while finite arithmetic and a bounded lattice impose further constraints. The update is deterministic; varied patterns are not evidence of random forcing. The experiment illustrates the interaction of local coupling, nonlinear iteration, and numerical precision, rather than a validated physical diffusion law or literally infinite state space.''', [('coupled','Direct original-Info reference for coupled map lattices and spatiotemporal patterns.'),('wolfram','Discrete-state cellular theory provides the comparison motivating continuous cell values.'),('chaos','Iterated maps and finite numerical approximations support interpretation of nonlinear local dynamics.')])
add('brians-brain', '''How does a refractory state alter the patterns supported by a local cellular rule? NetLogo Brian's Brain uses firing, refractory, and inactive states. Firing cells become refractory, refractory cells become inactive, and inactive cells fire when exactly two of their eight neighbors are firing. The original Info credits Brian Silverman for the rule. Wolfram [1] supplies cellular-automaton foundations, Greenberg and Hastings [2] study discrete excitable media, and Fisch and colleagues [3] analyze threshold and neighborhood effects in related systems. Counts are frozen before updates, preserving synchronous generations. The finite lattice, initialization, and exact two-neighbor birth condition shape moving configurations. This is a computational excitable pattern model rather than a physiological neuron simulation. Similar-looking waves in other systems motivate comparisons, but do not establish identical mechanisms or empirical validation.''', [('wolfram','General local-update theory supports the cellular-automaton interpretation.'),('excitable','Excitation followed by refractoriness is the directly relevant scientific analogy, while Brian’s rule is distinct.'),('excitable2','Threshold and range analysis explains why neighbor rules and state cycling influence propagating patterns.')])
add('cyclic-ca', '''How can cyclic local competition organize a disordered field into waves and spirals? NetLogo Cyclic CA assigns each patch a state on a color cycle. A patch advances only when enough neighbors occupy its successor state, using a selectable Moore or von Neumann neighborhood and radius. Fisch and colleagues [1] study threshold-range scaling in cyclic and excitable automata, Shalizi and Shalizi [2] examine how to quantify organization in cyclic systems, and Greenberg and Hastings [3] provide a related excitable-media comparison. The original model freezes states before updating to preserve simultaneous decisions. Threshold, state count, neighborhood geometry, and finite boundaries determine whether activity persists or freezes. Attractive spiral images illustrate local interaction and symmetry breaking; they do not by themselves quantify organization or establish a chemical, neural, or ecological explanation.''', [('excitable2','Directly cited original study links threshold, interaction radius and color cycling to collective patterns.'),('organization','Directly cited scientific treatment distinguishes measured organization from visual impressions.'),('excitable','A foundational related model clarifies the distinction between contact-driven cyclic changes and automatic refractory transitions.')])
add('ca-stochastic', '''How does uncertainty in a local transition change collective behavior? NetLogo CA Stochastic assigns a separate activation probability to each of the eight binary neighborhoods consisting of a cell and its immediate neighbors. Each generation is drawn as a new row, producing a one-dimensional space-time diagram. Wolfram [1] supplies the deterministic cellular foundation, Li and colleagues [2] study transitions across rule space, and Chaté and Manneville [3] analyze critical behavior. Setting probabilities to zero or one recovers deterministic cases, while intermediate values produce repeated runs with different realizations. Initial density, boundary conditions, and the eight probabilities jointly determine the observed patterns. The experiment illustrates stochastic update laws rather than merely uncertain initial conditions. A finite visual run cannot establish a critical point, a universal scaling law, or a faithful model of noisy natural processes.''', [('wolfram','Deterministic local rules supply the limiting cases when all probabilities are zero or one.'),('ca1','Transition phenomena motivate systematic parameter comparisons rather than judging a single image.'),('ca2','Directly cited criticality study provides the scientific framework and limits for interpreting apparent regime changes.')])
